# CSS codes, the Calderbank-Shor-Steane construction that turns a pair of nested classical codes into a quantum code. A
# classical code protects against bit flips only; a quantum code must protect against bit flips and phase flips at once,
# and the CSS trick is to use one classical code for the bit-flip errors and its dual for the phase-flip errors, so a
# single classical code that contains its own dual gives a quantum code for free. The Steane code is the CSS code built
# from the [7,4,3] Hamming code: seven physical qubits, one logical qubit, distance three, correcting any single-qubit
# error, written [[7,1,3]]. Its logical dimension is the Hamming dimension four minus the dual dimension three, one. The
# number of stabilizer generators is the physical count minus the logical count, seven minus one, six, so there are two to
# the sixth error syndromes, sixty-four, comfortably more than the twenty-two single-qubit errors it must distinguish, the
# identity plus three Pauli errors on each of seven qubits. The five-qubit code [[5,1,3]] is the smallest that corrects a
# single error and is PERFECT: its two to the fourth, sixteen, syndromes exactly equal the one identity plus three errors
# on each of five qubits, sixteen, so the quantum Hamming bound is saturated with no waste. This file checks the Steane
# dimension, its syndrome budget, and the perfect five-qubit saturation.

# The Steane logical dimension: the containing Hamming code has dimension 4, its dual has dimension 3, and the CSS logical
# count is the difference, 4 - 3 = 1, one protected qubit.
hold steane-encodes-one-logical-qubit
  call is-equal
    code 1
    call subtract
      code 4
      code 3

# The Steane code has 7 - 1 = 6 stabilizer generators, giving 2^6 = 64 error syndromes.
hold steane-has-sixty-four-syndromes
  call is-equal
    code 64
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            code 2
            call multiply
              code 2
              code 2

# The single-qubit errors the distance-three code must name: the identity plus three Pauli errors on each of seven qubits,
# 1 + 3*7 = 22, which fits inside the sixty-four syndromes, 22 below 64, so Steane is non-degenerate with room to spare.
hold steane-errors-fit-in-the-syndromes
  call is-below
    call add
      code 1
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 7
    code 64

# The five-qubit perfect code saturates the quantum Hamming bound: 2^(5-1) = 16 syndromes exactly equal the 1 + 3*5 = 16
# single-qubit error patterns, one identity plus three Paulis on each of five qubits, no syndrome wasted.
hold five-qubit-code-is-perfect
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          code 2
    call add
      code 1
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 5
